 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Overview",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "The TMP102 measures its own temperature and is aimed at thermal management and protection. Heat reaches the die mainly through the metal leads, which conduct far better than the moulded body. Production units are fully tested against NIST-traceable sensors, with equipment calibrated through ISO/IEC 17025 accredited labs. It suits replacing an NTC or PTC thermistor where accuracy matters, needs no lookup table or linearisation, and is specified over -40 °C to 125 °C. An automotive version, TMP102-Q1, is also qualified."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Temperature register",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Each conversion overwrites a read-only temperature register. Two bytes are read, most significant first; the top 12 bits (13 in extended mode) hold the reading and the second byte may be skipped if its bits are not wanted. Negative values are two's complement and one count is 0.0625 °C. Bit D0 of the second byte is 0 in normal mode and 1 in extended mode, so the format can be told from the data; other unused bits read 0. Until a first conversion has finished after power-up or reset, it holds 0 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Serial interface",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "The part is a bus target only and never drives SCL. SDA and SCL are open drain, with Schmitt triggers and spike filters on the inputs. Fast mode runs from 1 kHz to 400 kHz and high-speed mode from 1 kHz to 2.85 MHz; bytes go most significant bit first. A START is SDA falling while SCL is high, a STOP is SDA rising while SCL is high, and SDA must otherwise hold steady while SCL is high. The addressed receiver pulls SDA low on the ninth clock to acknowledge; the controller ends a read by not acknowledging. Any number of bytes may pass between START and STOP, and a write may stop after the first data byte to update only the upper byte."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Address",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "The seven-bit address is 10010 followed by two bits set by ADD0: 1001000 with ADD0 on ground, 1001001 on V+, 1001010 on SDA and 1001011 on SCL, so four sensors can share one bus."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Pointer, writes and reads",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "Every write starts with the address byte (R/W low) and a pointer byte whose two low bits, P1 and P0, pick the temperature (00), configuration (01), TLOW (10) or THIGH (11) register; P7 to P2 must be written 0. Data bytes that follow go into that register, each acknowledged. A read returns the register last pointed to, most significant byte first; to read another, write just the address and the new pointer, then issue a repeated START with R/W high. The pointer is kept between reads and is 00 (temperature) after power-up. Registers update byte by byte."
  },
  {
   "topic": "SMBus alert",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "In interrupt mode (TM = 1) ALERT can serve as an SMBus alert line. When the controller sends the alert response address (0001 1001), an alerting TMP102 acknowledges and returns its own address; the address byte's last bit tells which limit was crossed. With POL = 0 that bit is 0 for a temperature at or above THIGH and 1 for one below TLOW; POL = 1 inverts it. If several devices answer, the one with the lowest address wins arbitration and releases its ALERT; the losers keep theirs asserted."
  },
  {
   "topic": "General call",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "The part acknowledges the general-call address (000 0000 with the eighth bit 0). A second byte of 0000 0110 resets its registers to their power-up values. The general address acquire command is not supported."
  },
  {
   "topic": "High-speed mode",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "To go above 400 kHz the controller sends the HS master code 0000 1xxx straight after a START. The TMP102 does not acknowledge it but switches its SDA and SCL filters to high-speed operation, good to 2.85 MHz. A repeated START and then the target address follow. The bus stays in high-speed mode until a STOP, when the filters return to fast mode."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Timeout",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "Should SCL stay low for 30 ms typical inside a transaction, the part resets its interface, lets go of SDA and waits for a new START. Keeping SCL at 1 kHz or faster avoids tripping it."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Continuous conversion and start-up",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "By default the part converts continuously, each result replacing the last. A conversion takes about 10 ms; the part then powers down until the next one is due, at 0.25, 1, 4 (default) or 8 per second set by CR1 and CR0. A conversion starts at once after power-up or a general-call reset, so the first result is ready about 10 ms later. Supply current is about 55 µA during a conversion and about 2.6 µA in the wait between (typical, at 27 °C)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Extended mode (EM)",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "EM = 0 (normal) uses a 12-bit format for the temperature, THIGH and TLOW registers, matching the TMP75. EM = 1 (extended) uses 13 bits for all three so readings above 128 °C can be held."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Shutdown (SD)",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "SD = 1 stops everything except the serial interface once the conversion under way completes; current falls to the shutdown figure, 0.15 µA typical. SD = 0 returns to continuous conversion. Entering shutdown also clears an interrupt-mode ALERT."
  },
  {
   "topic": "One-shot (OS)",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "In shutdown, writing OS = 1 starts one conversion; OS reads 0 while it runs and 1 when it is done, and the part goes back to shutdown. This saves power where readings are needed only now and then. With a conversion of about 10 ms and a read under 20 µs, one-shot use allows 80 or more conversions per second."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Thermostat mode (TM) and ALERT",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "The limits sit in THIGH and TLOW, coded like the temperature register, and are compared after every conversion. Comparator mode (TM = 0): ALERT asserts once the temperature is at or above THIGH for the set number of consecutive conversions and releases once it is below TLOW for that many. Interrupt mode (TM = 1): ALERT asserts when the temperature reaches THIGH for the set count and stays asserted until any register is read, the part answers an SMBus alert response, or it is shut down; it then asserts again only when the temperature drops below TLOW, is cleared the same way, and the cycle repeats. A general-call reset also clears ALERT and returns the part to comparator mode. Power-up limits are THIGH = 80 °C and TLOW = 75 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Polarity (POL)",
   "page": 17,
   "text": "POL = 0 (default) makes ALERT active low; POL = 1 makes it active high."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Fault queue (F1, F0)",
   "page": 18,
   "text": "A fault is a conversion outside the limits. To reject noise, ALERT acts only after 1, 2, 4 or 6 faults in a row, chosen by F1 and F0 (00, 01, 10, 11)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Converter resolution (R1, R0)",
   "page": 18,
   "text": "R1 and R0 are read only and read 11 from start-up, meaning 12-bit resolution."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Alert bit (AL)",
   "page": 19,
   "text": "AL is read only and reports the comparator state whatever TM is. With POL = 0 it reads 1 until the temperature has been at or above THIGH for the set number of faults, then 0 until it has been below TLOW for that many, then 1 again. POL = 1 inverts it."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Use",
   "page": 20,
   "text": "The part reads the temperature of the board where it is soldered; four of them on one bus can watch four spots on a board."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Parts around it",
   "page": 20,
   "text": "SCL, SDA and ALERT each need a pull-up; 5 kΩ is recommended, and other values are fine so long as no pin sinks more than 3 mA. SCL and SDA may be pulled to a rail at or above V+. A 0.01 µF bypass capacitor is required on V+, placed as near the V+ and GND pins as possible; noisy or high-impedance supplies may need more decoupling. The part is optimised for 3.3 V but measures accurately across 1.4 V to 3.6 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply filter",
   "page": 21,
   "text": "The part draws little and puts little noise on its supply. An RC filter on V+, a series resistor of less than 5 kΩ and a capacitor of more than 10 nF, cuts that noise further."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Placement and thermal coupling",
   "page": 20,
   "text": "Put the sensor close to the heat source it watches, with a layout that couples it well, so changes show quickly. For air or surface measurement, keep the package and leads apart from the surrounding air; thermally conductive adhesive helps a surface reading. The step response's time constant depends on the board the part is mounted on."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout",
   "page": 22,
   "text": "The bypass capacitor goes as close as possible to the V+ and GND pins, with vias to the power or ground plane; SCL, SDA and ALERT are pulled up through 5 kΩ resistors. Extra decoupling can be added for noisy or high-impedance supplies. A ground plane under the part couples it thermally to the heat source."
  }
 ],
